  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The evidence of sustained interaction and ecological dependency is reconstructed from a single archaeological site complex.",
      "what": "Archaeological excavations at Juukan Gorge recovered the first physical evidence of Tasmanian devils in northwestern Australia's Pilbara region, consisting of a Holocene dentary fragment and a pre-LGM Late Pleistocene premolar. Analysis of these specimens and associated trace fossils, such as chewed and digested bones, documents a continuous human-devil interaction spanning at least 45,000 years until the species went extinct on the mainland approximately 3,200 years ago.",
      "why": "The findings establish that Tasmanian devils inhabited arid inland environments and maintained a long-term ecological relationship with humans, scavenging on butchery waste from the moment of human arrival until the introduction of dingoes disrupted this dynamic."
    }
